## 3. TECHNOLOGY AND DEPLOYMENT

Use:
- TypeScript and Node.js.
- A maintained HTTP client.
- A robust HTML parser such as Cheerio when HTML parsing is necessary.
- SHA-256 for deterministic notice fingerprints.
- The official GitHub API for persistent state and repository history.
- The Telegram Bot API for notifications.
- Google AI Studio Build mode for development.
- Google Cloud Run or a compatible persistent serverless HTTP host for production execution.
- cron-job.org as the external scheduler.

The scraper must execute on the server. Never perform scraping, state mutation, or Telegram token usage in frontend browser code.

The application must expose authenticated endpoints:
- GET /health
- POST /api/cron/run
- POST /api/test/all-zones
- POST /api/test/zone
- POST /api/test/telegram
- POST /api/admin/bootstrap
- POST /api/admin/dry-run

Protect every action endpoint using a strong server-side bearer secret or equivalent authentication. Validate authorization before any state changes or Telegram delivery.

The health endpoint may expose non-sensitive service status but must never reveal credentials, private state contents, or internal secrets.

Configure timeouts, request limits, controlled concurrency, retries with exponential backoff and jitter, and bounded memory use.

Because cron-job.org has an approximately 30-second execution timeout, design the scheduled request to complete safely within the available time. Measure the actual worst-case runtime. If a full scan cannot complete safely, implement a persistent, resumable scan queue and schedule bounded batches with explicit progress tracking. Do not report a partial scan as a successful full scan.

Avoid overlapping executions using a durable lock or another reliable concurrency-control mechanism. A process-local mutex alone is insufficient for multiple server instances.

